<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Beijing</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Beijing is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> that has served as the capital of several imperial dynasties. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadu">Dadu</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanbaliq">Khanbaliq</a>) it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> moved its capital there in the early fifteenth century, building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>. Its fall to rebels and then to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> in 1644 marked the end of the Ming and the foundation of Qing rule in China.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/beijing.jpg" alt="Beijing" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: N509FZ · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skyline_of_Beijing_CBD_with_B-5906_approaching_(20211016171955)_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Beijing is situated at the northern edge of the roughly triangular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-china-plain">North China Plain</a>, which opens to the south and east of the city. Mountains to the north, northwest and west shield the city and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s agricultural heartland from the encroaching desert steppes. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall of China</a> across the northern part of the municipality was built on the rugged topography to defend against nomadic incursions from the steppes. The highest point of the municipality is Mount Dongling, at 2,303 metres, in the Western Hills on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>.</p><p>The name Beijing means 'Northern Capital' and was applied to the city in 1403, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>, to distinguish it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, the 'Southern Capital'. The older English spelling Peking was used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary Martino Martini in an atlas published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> in 1655. Over the past 3,000 years the city has had numerous other names.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest traces of human habitation in the municipality were found in the caves of Dragon Bone Hill near the village of Zhoukoudian, where Peking Man lived. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a> fossils from the caves date to 230,000 to 250,000 years ago, and Homo sapiens lived there more recently, about 27,000 years ago. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> settlements have been found throughout the municipality, including in Wangfujing in central Beijing.</p><p>The first walled city in the area was Ji, the capital of the state of Ji, located around the present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guan-people">Guang</a>'anmen area in the south of Xicheng District. During the early Zhou period the settlement was conquered by the state of Yan, which made it its new capital. Its original capital lay 45 kilometres to the southwest in Fangshan District. Yan eventually became one of the seven major states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Beijing is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and is governed as a provincial-level municipality with 16 districts. In 2024 its population was about 21.8 million. Its traditional housing consists of siheyuans, courtyard houses, and the narrow alleys between them called hutongs. It has eight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/forbidden-city">Forbidden City</a>, the Temple of Heaven, the Summer Palace, the Ming Tombs, the Zhoukoudian Peking Man Site and parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. Beijing opera is its best-known traditional form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Beijing” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1960 the North made war on the Republic of Vietnam through the Viet Cong, sending troops and supplies down the Ho Chi Minh trail, and in 1962 it intensified the infiltration of men and materiel, while <b>Beijing</b> supplied the Viet Cong with small arms and heavier weapons.</blockquote><a href="/empire/democratic-republic-of-vietnam">Democratic Republic of Vietnam · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Ahmad Shah's envoy Khwaja Mirhan reached <b>Beijing</b> in 1763 he refused to kowtow to the Qianlong Emperor; the Qing read it as a claim of equality and cut ties with the Afghans, though the envoy himself was shown favour.</blockquote><a href="/empire/durrani-empire">Durrani Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Ulan Butung in 1690, a few hundred kilometres north of <b>Beijing</b>, Galdan's army sheltered behind a wall of kneeling camels and escaped, but in 1696 Kangxi led a great expedition into Mongolia and a Qing army under Fiyanggu destroyed Galdan's forces at Jao Modo;…</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1120 Jin concluded the 'Alliance Conducted at Sea' with the Song dynasty against Liao, and Jin armies took the Liao Supreme Capital that year and <b>Yanjing</b> (Beijing) in 1122, after Song forces had failed to capture it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jin-dynasty-1115-1234">Jin dynasty (1115-1234) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Foreign trade was tightly limited to tribute missions to <b>Beijing</b> and the Japanese trading post at Busan, run by the Sō clan of Tsushima.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Expansion northwards provoked the Sino-Nepalese War of 1788–1792; in 1792 a Qing army under Fuk’anggan advanced close to Kathmandu and forced Nepal to accept peace and send regular tribute missions to <b>Beijing</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Military</a></li></ul></section>
